#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069783} :activity/id #uuid "8a5dbd8f-ec74-5978-b2ea-7e2cb96c5a6b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048697} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069783 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070003 :tutorial/content "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = e^{x - y}\\,.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of both $x$ and $y,$ so we can't solve the equation by integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ at least not straight away. So, how do we find a general solution to the equation?\n\nFirst of all, note that it is possible to separate the variables $x$ and $y$ on each side of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} &={e}^{x-y}=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{y}}\\,⟹\\,{e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the rule for integration by substitution, we can simplify the integrand on the left-hand side and carry out the integration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & ={e}^{x}+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ne^y  \t= e^x + C.\n$$\n\nThis is an **implicit form** of the solution since it is not written explicitly as $y=y(x).$\n\nWe can write our general solution in **explicit form** (i.e., in the form $y=y(x)$) by noting that since $e^y > 0$ for all $y$, we may assume that the right-hand side is positive and apply the natural logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x}+C) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x}+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dfb65111-b319-54c6-84cc-f60506595a69" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6766 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "15fb7623-3ae9-5536-a894-f9499872d39a" :step/math-academy-id 41051 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069784}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069784 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070004 :tutorial/content "The differential equation that we just solved was **separable**. A first-order differential equation is separable if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} = h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{g(y)} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = h(x).\n$$\n\nOnce the variables are separated, we can integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{g(y)} & =\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then evaluate both integrals and find the solution. Note that for the solution to be valid, we require $g(y)\\neq 0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "56fc1c96-3599-5022-8d83-60d4188ccf07" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6767 :tutorial/title "First-Order Separable Equations"} :step/id #uuid "a58fbcf9-b208-5301-bab1-7af13f7e4794" :step/math-academy-id 41052 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069787}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069785 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068840 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068447 :question/id #uuid "d1de6657-82b1-5bcb-b8cc-fd2629d31faa" :question/math-academy-id "e-10832" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y' = \\dfrac {x^2}{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}y^2 = \\dfrac{1}{3}x^3 + C.\n$$\n\nWe can express the solution in the form $y = y(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+C \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+2C \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+2C} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+K}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K = 2C.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16f0876a-33f0-5c6b-9195-8b9a0a550655" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551645} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551637} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186623673}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Explicit Form"} :step/id #uuid "236e7d75-2463-579e-b10b-333f79db27f2" :step/math-academy-id 41053 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069786}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069786 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068841 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068448 :question/id #uuid "23d66cc8-8e5a-5a33-a7a1-c43f5facdcbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-10833" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y' + 2xy = 0$ for $y\\neq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2xy & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2xy \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-{x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\ln \\vert y \\vert = -x^2 + C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y = y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-{x}^{2}+C \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ |y| & =K{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K = e^C$  and $K > 0.$ \n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ke^{-x^2}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b784e1-ad0e-5cf2-8b02-604a7098c6a9" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554818} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615416}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions by Rearranging Then Integrating"} :step/id #uuid "a5b45f96-1870-5374-8c25-edaa724ee982" :step/math-academy-id 41056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069787 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068842 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068449 :question/id #uuid "32040154-373e-5a10-a460-1d17f6989da0" :question/math-academy-id "e-10849" :question/problem "Given that $x,y \\geq 0,$ separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\sqrt{2xy}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differentiable equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{g(y)} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differentiable equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =  \\sqrt{2x} \\cdot  \\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{y}} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\sqrt{2x}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0d908f1-591e-5cb6-afc5-fa7ec44ec326" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049380}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671838} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671837}] :knowledge-point/title "Separating the Variables in a Differential Equation"} :step/id #uuid "61c964d8-24bd-5f08-a5da-5f3b94e5fe79" :step/math-academy-id 41115 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069789}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069789 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068844 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068451 :question/id #uuid "d53c6172-978d-57c7-b1f0-e56e892e48a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-10997" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    \\dfrac{x}{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+K \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+2K \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C= 2K.$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\ny^2 = x^2 + C .\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67b149c-f701-5be0-868d-c2f4a4be4be4" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564796} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564787}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Implicit Form"} :step/id #uuid "2be8f97d-22ae-5ccc-a9cc-96d65939ade1" :step/math-academy-id 41758 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069785}}] :activity/title "Solving First-Order ODEs Using Separation of Variables" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068992}]}
